{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ watermelons (+ scarce shortage shortages) (+ because due caused) (+ weather drought rain crop harvest))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_43_387210468#1_850526519","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2737","rank":1,"snippet":"cultivation practices or from a specific malady. In most cases, the problem is treatable and future fruits can be rescued. Advertisement Blossom End Rot If the blossom end of the watermelon turns brown and appears to be collapsing inward, the culprit may be blossom end rot. Blossom end rot occurs when rainfall is particularly <cover>scarce. Mulch the base of the vines to help keep the roots moist and cool, and water regularly to prevent future occurrences of blossom end rot. Remove the damaged fruit. Use care around the vine when cutting out weeds or spreading fertilizer to avoid injuring the vine. Rather than a conventional mulch, try using black plastic around the base of the plant to retain moisture and block weeds. Advertisement Belly Rot Belly rot is caused by a fungus, Rhizoctonia solani Kuhn, and is most prevalent during warm, humid or unusually rainy weather.\" , \"start_char\": 433.000000 , \"title\": \"Why Do Watermelons </cover>Rot on the Vines As They Are Growing? | Hunker\" , \"url\": \"https://www.hunker.com/12547071/why-do-watermelons-rot-on-the-vines-as-they-are-growing\" }"}}
